//
// First, we instantiate a `std::random_device`, which serves as an
// entropy source and yields unpredictable values when supported by
// the platform (see [7]).
//
// Next, we use that entropy to seed a Mersenne Twister engine,
// `std::mt19937`, a high-quality pseudo-random number generator
// (referenced at [8]). Other engines may be chosen based on
// performance or statistical requirements (see [alternative engines]).
//
// A `std::uniform_int_distribution` is constructed to map outcomes
// uniformly across the integers 1 through 6 (inclusive), ensuring
// each face has equal probability (as documented in [9]).
//
// Invoking `dice_range(generator)` produces a single random outcome
// conforming to this distribution ([11]), emulating the roll of a
// physical die.
//
// Additional rolls can be obtained by calling `dice_range(generator)`
